verdicts.why:
The cited function does the header half and NOT the cap half. files.ts:64-67 `renderInstructions(files)` returns `files.map(f => `### ${f.displayPath}

${f.content.trim()}`).join("

")` (with an early `if (files.length === 0) return ""` at :65) — so the per-file workspace-relative header and blank-line join are exactly as claimed (displayPath is computed relative to the workspace at :56). But there is NO maxBytes parameter and NO truncation anywhere in this file: the byte cap and the literal '(truncated)' suffix live in the sibling index.ts: `createInstructionsSection` (:23) resolves `config.maxBytes ?? DEFAULT_INSTRUCTIONS_MAX_BYTES` (:24, default 24_000 at :9) and does `if (text.length > maxBytes) text = text.slice(0, maxBytes) + "(truncated)"` (:48-50). Also note the cap is by UTF-16 code units (`.length`), not bytes, despite the config name. So the claim's final sentence rests entirely on the sibling module, and the closing rationale ('bounded prompt cost for an unbounded set of on-disk files') is a design assertion the cited function does not enforce.
